  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nMid-level high pressure... That's been the statement of the summer. \nWhile we continue this trend through the forecast period, closer to \nthe surface, there is a tad bit more going on. Across the western \nGulf, the tropical moisture that was advected into Texas in the past \nweek has dispersed leaving much of the region with PWAT values below \nthe 50th percentile for this time of year (1.3-1.5\"). For that \nreason, there is higher confidence that seabreeze won't be as active \nfor the remainder of the afternoon. A few streamer showers may push \ntoward the upper Coastal Bend Sunday morning, and while PWATs climb \nback up to 1.7-2.0\", there is not much support for organized \nshowers/storms in terms of a lifting mechanism. Monday has a \nslightly better chance for showers thanks to any residual outflow \nfrom storms in North Texas approaching the Texas coast. The \nconvergence of weak outflow winds approaching the seabreeze may \ntrigger the seabreeze to be more active, but general confidence is \nlow and thus capped PoPs Monday to 15-20%, with similar percentages \nalong the coastal (seabreeze) through Wednesday.\n\nBeyond Wednesday, global models are showing a bit more northeasterly \nwinds across much of the Southern US with a stronger NE to SW \ngradient over the Gulf. This fetch across the Gulf will gradually \nincrease seas (3-4 ft), but additionally, this wind direction brings \nSLIGHTLY drier air through the coastal plains. For this reason, \nwhile there are 20-40% PoPs from Wednesday across South Texas, there \nis low confidence that this will actually play out due to a wetter \nbias from the NBM. My suspicion is that these PoPs will trend drier \nwith subsequent model runs, but this will be dependent on the \nstrength of the gradient across the Gulf. South of this easterly \nflow, Caribbean tropical moisture will round out the Yucatan \nPeninsula with the northeasterly flow bringing this moisture (and \ngreater rain chances) more towards Mexico's Gulf coastline and \ncloser to deep South Texas. \n\nWhat will be guaranteed with high confidence is the 90/low 100 \ndegree weather remaining in place through the forecast period. \nBefore the slightly drier air moves in mid-week, the HeatRisk will \nclimb to Major (level 3 of 4) with isolated areas of Extreme. Beyond \nmid-week, the overall extent of Major HeatRisk spatially decreases. \nThat's the best Fall weather I can see on my side of things for now. \nYou're more than welcome to autumnal-related drinks... as long as \nyou step into the coffee shop for AC relief (but don't forget to \nsupplement that hot drink with water for your hydration)!",
